      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14163281/assets/1a24187f280eb99640c3d1bfae3b7fe873383f04c2c492e7873a40de16a9560b.png)\n\nFind the measure of angle $C$ given that $m\\angle{A} = m\\angle{B}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Using $m\\angle{A} = m\\angle{B},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A & =m∠B \\\\ 3x-{27}^{∘} & =2x \\\\ x & ={27}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, substituting $x=27^\\circ$ in the expression for $m\\angle B,$ we get\n\n$$\nm\\angle{B}=2(27^\\circ)=54^\\circ.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find $m\\angle C$ using the fact that the internal angles of any triangle sum to $180^\\circ$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2\\cdot {54}^{∘}+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {108}^{∘}+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠C & ={72}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
